# Implement

Use this skill to implement the planned change step by step.

## Rule preflight

- Before doing anything else in this skill, read:
  - `{{ manifesto_path }}`
  - `ai.config.yaml`
  - `openspec/config.yaml`
  - `.ai/context/current.md` if it exists
  - `.ai/rules/workflow.mdc`
  - `.ai/rules/workflow-gates.mdc`
  - `.ai/rules/project-profile.mdc`
{% if minimalism_enabled and minimalism_level != "off" %}
  - `.ai/rules/minimal-solution.mdc`
{% endif %}
- Treat those files as hard constraints for this run, not background reference.
- Do not continue with implementation until this preflight read is complete.
- Skipping preflight or treating rules as optional background is a workflow defect.

## Project rules compliance (mandatory)

After Rule preflight, **before the first code change**:

1. Extract every bullet from `.ai/rules/project-profile.mdc` → `## Project-Specific Rules`.
2. Extract manifesto and workflow-gate constraints that apply to the touched scope.
3. Add `## Project Rules Compliance` to `progress.md` — one line per rule:
   - `- [ ] <rule text> — evidence: pending`
4. On every step transition and before `/review` handoff:
   - re-read `.ai/rules/project-profile.mdc`
   - update each line to `[x]` with concrete evidence (`file:line`, test, command) or `N/A (reason)` if truly out of scope
5. An unchecked applicable rule or a known violation blocks progress — fix code or update the plan first; do not hand off to review with open violations.

If `## Project-Specific Rules` is empty, still record explicit compliance checks for manifesto, `ai.config.yaml`, and workflow gates in `## Project Rules Compliance`.

## Mandatory protocol

1. Read `{{ manifesto_path }}` and `ai.config.yaml`.
2. Use model tier `{{ agent_model_implement }}` (`.ai/reference/model-profiles.md`).
3. Read `.ai/reference/context-artifacts.md` and `.ai/reference/model-profiles.md`.
4. Before changing code, load the relevant context and existing implementation surfaces:
   - `.ai/context/current.md`
   - the selected OpenSpec change and relevant repository files
   - nearby canonical code that can be reused instead of reimplemented
5. Select exactly one `openspec/changes/<change-id>/` and read its proposal, delta specs, design, and tasks.
6. Run `openspec instructions apply --change <change-id>` and treat the resulting instructions plus the selected change artifacts as the implementation contract.
7. Do not start medium/large work until OpenSpec reports the change implementation-ready and `openspec validate <change-id>` passes.
8. Before changing code, extract from the OpenSpec change:
   - scope and explicit non-goals from `proposal.md`
   - normative requirements and scenarios from delta specs
   - architecture decisions, invariants, migrations, and rollback from `design.md`
   - current task boundaries and verification mapping from `tasks.md`
{% if minimalism_enabled and minimalism_level != "off" %}
8a. **Before the first code change**, re-run the minimal solution ladder against the actual codebase. If a simpler approach works:
   - update `design.md` and `tasks.md` before coding
   - record the decision in `progress.md` → `## Minimal Solution Conformance`
{% endif %}
9. Before the first code change, create or update execution memory in `{{ plan_progress_runtime_root }}/ISSUE-ID/progress.md` only (local `PROGRESS::ISSUE-ID` artifact; never mirror progress body into Linear comments).
9. Execute strictly step by step. On every step transition:
   - update `## Выполнено`
   - update `## Текущий шаг`
   - update `## Дальше`
   - update `## Spec Conformance`
   - update `## Project Rules Compliance` (per-rule pass/N/A with evidence)
   - append short decisions to `## Решения по ходу`
10. Implement abstraction-first **and** minimal-solution aligned:
   - prefer extending existing abstractions over introducing parallel branches
   - do not add a new abstraction layer with one implementation (see `.ai/rules/minimal-solution.mdc`)
   - prefer strategy/polymorphism/composition/data-driven dispatch over long `if/else` ladders
   - if branching starts growing, extract an explicit abstraction instead of stacking conditionals
{% if minimalism_enabled and minimalism_level != "off" %}
   - mark deliberate simplifications with `ponytail:` comments per minimal-solution rule
{% endif %}
11. For database or query-heavy paths, keep the implementation production-grade:
   - avoid N+1 lookups
   - avoid fetch-all + filter/map/reduce loops when the storage/query layer can do the work
   - return only the fields and rows actually required
   - avoid in-memory post-processing of oversized datasets
12. Use canonical tasks instead of stack-specific guesses:
   - `{{ task_command }} {{ task_lint_name }}`
   - `{{ task_command }} {{ task_test_name }}`
   - `{{ task_command }} {{ task_verify_name }}`
13. Testing policy:
   - under `/orchestrator`, run **scoped** tests only until the orchestrator runs the single end-of-pipeline full verify (see `.ai/reference/orchestrator-test-strategy.md`)
   - default to the smallest credible test scope that covers the touched behavior
   - do not assume the full project suite is required unless the user explicitly asks for it or repository rules require it
   - if scoped testing is not enough because the change crosses broad/shared surfaces, say that and expand the scope deliberately
   - if an orchestrator run has `Claimed Write Scope` and full `{{ task_command }} {{ task_lint_name }}` / `{{ task_command }} {{ task_verify_name }}` fails only outside that scope, record command output + rationale in `progress.md` and still attach scoped green evidence for the task; do not stall review solely on repo-wide red (orchestrator skill defines the bar)
14. If implementation drifts from the source request or OpenSpec artifacts:
   - either fix the code
   - or update the OpenSpec change first with `/opsx:update`
   - otherwise stop
14. If `.ai/runtime/orchestrator-state.mjs` has an active run for the issue:
   - use only the assigned orchestrator worktree
   - do not create a second branch/worktree
   - update run state through the orchestrator runtime helper
15. All reasoning, progress notes, and user-facing workflow dialogue must follow workflow language `{{ workflow_language }}` unless the user explicitly requests another language.
16. If the latest review produced `high` or `medium` findings, implementation must resume from those findings and continue the loop:
   - fix the findings
   - run `{{ task_command }} {{ task_test_name }}`
   - run `{{ task_command }} {{ task_verify_name }}`
   - run `/review` again
   - do not consider the task complete until review no longer reports `high` or `medium`
17. Keep the active OpenSpec change coherent during implementation:
   - check off completed entries in `tasks.md` as evidence is produced
   - Use `/opsx:update <change-id>` when implementation changes the approved artifacts; update proposal/specs/design/tasks before continuing code work
   - run `openspec validate <change-id>` before review handoff
   - Do not run `/opsx:sync` or archive the change from `/implement`; review and final full verification have not completed yet
{% if linear_enabled %}
18. When execution starts and Linear MCP is available, move the issue workflow state to `{{ tracker_status_active }}` per tracker rules (state only — no progress comment sync).
{% else %}
18. Do not change Linear issue state; execution state lives in local artifacts only.
{% endif %}

## Do not do this

- Do not silently invent extra scope.
- Do not write a long diary instead of a progress artifact.
- Do not bypass canonical tasks with direct stack commands in prompts or status updates.
- Do not ignore reusable code that was already discoverable through local code reading.
- Do not solve growing complexity with another layer of ad-hoc `if/else`.
